{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015mFg6irdNjpbbMuScn7aNQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A1jqmNyAiPTgFE9pBQZMqE","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n% ============================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume that item difficulty is stable across model generations---when we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores measure the same construct.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that \\textbf{31.3\\%} of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts---a rate 142$\\times$ above the permutation null ($p<0.001$, $z=622$).\nThis instability is robust across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%), not explained by capability improvement ($\\leq$2.4\\,pp of C\\%; $R^2=0.0007$), and not driven by model non-independence or architecture heterogeneity.\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection (enrichment $\\approx$1.0, $\\chi^2$ $p=0.258$), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all tested subjects.\nDespite 31\\% instability, MMLU rankings remain robust ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), but DIF-item removal reveals systematic cohort bias ($z=8.5$)---DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding \\emph{which} capability dimensions shift across generations, not a quality filter.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n% ============================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nLLM evaluation relies heavily on static benchmarks whose items are assumed to function identically regardless of when or which model is evaluated.\nThis assumption---\\emph{temporal measurement invariance}---is rarely tested, yet its violation would undermine cross-generational score comparability.\nPsychometrics has a mature tool for exactly this purpose: \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\citep{holland1993differential}.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to METABENCH \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, a repository of model-level response matrices, analyzing 5,227 models on 12,508 MMLU \\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} items.\nThis is the largest DIF analysis conducted on LLM benchmarks.\nOur key narrative principle: \\textbf{instability $\\neq$ invalidity}.\nDIF flags items whose functioning changed across model generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\n\nWe make four contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Temporal instability characterization}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large DIF between 2023 and 2024 cohorts, robust to threshold choice (10--49\\%), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3,918), and systematic confound elimination (architecture, ability gap, local dependence, model non-independence).\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Their enrichment $\\approx$1.0 against DIF flags is expected, not failure. Any item-level detector validated against these labels has this blind spot.\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818).\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z=-9.5$ to $-49\\sigma$) but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy. Removing DIF items reveals systematic cohort bias ($z=8.5$), demonstrating actionable diagnostics for benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nToken-level methods (Min-K\\%: AUROC$=$0.72 on SFT, degrading to 0.51 on reasoning-RL models) and statistical approaches (ConStat: AUROC$=$0.62; DVD: AUROC$=$0.58--0.71) detect memorization but target different signals than DIF.\nConStat's perturbation-sensitivity approach and DVD's generation-variance method are orthogonal to DIF's cohort-differential signal by construction (Jaccard overlap$=$0.15 between DIF and ConStat flags).\n\n\\paragraph{Web-overlap ground truth.}\n\\citet{li2024contamination} provide web-overlap labels for MMLU items, classifying whether items appear in public training corpora.\nThese labels have become the \\emph{de facto} ground truth for contamination detection validation, but they measure global exposure rather than differential contamination---a distinction we formalize in \\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}\nMH-DIF \\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988alternate} is the standard procedure for detecting items that function differently across demographic groups in high-stakes testing.\nThe ETS classification system (A/B/C) combines statistical significance with effect-size thresholds \\citep{zieky1993practical}.\nWe apply this framework without modification to LLM model cohorts, treating release date as the grouping variable.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark evaluation methodology.}\nGrowing Pains \\citep{polo2024growing} uses anchor-item designs for efficient LLM evaluation.\nOur DIF analysis is complementary: it identifies which anchor items are degrading over time.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{MH-DIF for LLM Benchmarks}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times I}$ (models $\\times$ items), we partition models into reference (2023) and focal (2024-H1) cohorts.\nFor each item $i$, the Mantel--Haenszel statistic tests whether the odds of correct response differ between cohorts after stratifying by total score (ability proxy).\nThe MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ is converted to the $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ effect-size metric on the ETS delta scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified per ETS guidelines: Category~A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|<1.0$ or not significant), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ and significant), C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ and significant).\nCategory~C items exhibit large DIF warranting review.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\nTo validate DIF detection power under controlled conditions, we inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor a subset of focal-cohort models and target items, we flip incorrect responses to correct with probability $p$ (exploitation rate), simulating contamination at controlled intensity.\nWe measure $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$ and $\\Delta$FPR $=$ FPR$(p)$ $-$ FPR$(0)$.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}\n\\label{sec:crosssubject}\n\nStandard MH-DIF uses total score as the ability matching variable.\nWhen contamination affects the same items used for matching, the matching variable itself becomes contaminated, inflating FPR.\nWe propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: for items in subject $s$, use total score on all subjects \\emph{except} $s$ as the matching variable.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination at the cost of slightly noisier ability estimation.\n\n\\textbf{Proposition 1.} Under cross-subject matching, injecting contamination into subject $s$ items does not alter the matching variable for those items.\nTherefore $\\Delta$FPR $= 0$ for the contaminated subject, regardless of exploitation rate $p$.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal}\n\nWe analyze the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohorts: reference ($N_{\\text{ref}}=1{,}080$, released before 2024) and focal ($N_{\\text{foc}}=807$, released 2024-H1).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU. C\\% denotes the percentage of items classified as ETS Category~C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). Random C\\% is from within-cohort random splits.}\n\\label{tab:temporal}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & A\\% & B\\% & C\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal 2023 vs.\\ 2024 & 1,080 & 807 & 45.3 & 23.4 & 31.3 \\\\\nAbility Q1 vs.\\ Q5 & 2,608 & 2,608 & 82.1 & 15.0 & 2.9 \\\\\nWithin-family Llama-2 vs.\\ 3 & 161 & 308 & 18.7 & 20.0 & 61.3 \\\\\n\\midrule\nRandom split (sanity) & -- & -- & -- & -- & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal} presents the primary results.\nThe temporal cohort shows 31.3\\% C-rate items (3,918 items; 95\\% bootstrap CI: [29.5, 33.1], $n=1{,}000$).\nThe random-split sanity check yields C\\%$=$0.18\\%, confirming that the MH procedure itself does not inflate false positives.\nThe ability-quintile cohort (Q1 vs.\\ Q5) shows only 2.9\\% C-rate (enrichment vs.\\ local independence$=$1.19), demonstrating that raw ability differences alone produce minimal DIF.\nThe within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3, $d=-0.99$) yields 61.3\\%, providing an informative contrast for the ability-confound analysis in \\S\\ref{sec:confounds}.\n\nAll 57 MMLU subjects exhibit DIF (C\\% range: 20.7--48.4\\%, mean$=$30.6\\%, $\\text{SD}=5.5$; Kruskal--Wallis $p=0.78$), confirming benchmark-wide instability rather than subject-specific artifacts.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Domain-level DIF distribution (top/bottom subjects by C\\%).}\n\\label{tab:domain}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}\n\\toprule\nSubject & $N$ & C\\% & \\%Fav.\\ 2024 & Domain \\\\\n\\midrule\nbusiness\\_ethics & 100 & 48.4 & 38.0 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\nhuman\\_aging & 223 & 41.9 & 34.5 & Other \\\\\nformal\\_logic & 126 & 40.4 & 42.1 & Humanities \\\\\nhigh\\_school\\_stats & 216 & 42.0 & 55.3 & STEM \\\\\n\\midrule\ncomputer\\_security & 100 & 20.7 & 48.1 & STEM \\\\\nmarketing & 234 & 20.9 & 40.2 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\nmanagement & 103 & 21.7 & 43.7 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:domain} shows that high-DIF and low-DIF subjects span all domains.\nA modest subject-level correlation exists between DIF rate and domain accuracy improvement ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$, $n=57$), but item-level decomposition (\\S\\ref{sec:confounds}) shows this is largely a Simpson's paradox artifact.\n\nSTEM subjects show higher rates of 2024-favoring DIF (55.4\\%) versus Humanities (35.6\\%), revealing domain-specific capability evolution patterns.\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null and Statistical Robustness}\n\\label{sec:permutation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Permutation null distribution ($N=1{,}000$) vs.\\ observed temporal C\\%.}\n\\label{tab:permutation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nObserved C\\% & 31.3 \\\\\nPermutation null mean & 0.22 \\\\\nPermutation null SD & 0.05 \\\\\nPermutation null max & 0.41 \\\\\n$z$-score & 622.0 \\\\\n$p$-value & $<0.001$ \\\\\nRandom-split control & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe randomly reassign temporal labels 1,000 times (preserving $N_{\\text{ref}}=1{,}080$, $N_{\\text{foc}}=807$) and rerun MH-DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:permutation}).\nThe observed 31.3\\% never appears in any permutation (null max$=$0.41\\%), yielding $z=622$ ($p<0.001$).\nThe null mean (0.22\\%) closely matches the random-split control (0.18\\%), confirming both as valid null estimates.\n\nThreshold sensitivity analysis shows C\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ thresholds \\{1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5\\} $=$ \\{48.9\\%, 31.3\\%, 18.3\\%, 10.2\\%\\}.\nBenjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q=0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C items (31.32\\%$\\to$31.30\\%), confirming that the ETS dual requirement (effect size $+$ significance) provides near-complete implicit multiple-testing protection.\n\n\\subsection{Systematic Confound Elimination}\n\\label{sec:confounds}\n\nWe systematically evaluate six potential confounds.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture heterogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227).\nRestricting to decoder-only models yields identical results (C\\%$=$31.3\\%, Jaccard$=$1.0, 0 items changed).\n\n\\paragraph{Ability confound.}\nThe temporal cohort ability gap is small (Cohen's $d=-0.17$, overlap$=$76.4\\%).\nNearest-neighbor 1:1 matching ($d \\approx -0.01$) reduces C\\% from 31.3\\% to 28.9\\%$\\pm$0.1\\%---ability confound accounts for only 2.4\\,pp (7.7\\%).\nThe within-family Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 contrast ($d=-0.99$) drops from 61.3\\% to 28.3\\% under matching.\nBoth analyses converge to $\\sim$28--29\\% genuine item-level DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Local independence.}\nYen's Q3 statistics show negligible local dependence (mean Q3$=$0.009 within-subject, 2.2\\% of cross-subject pairs above 0.20).\nRemoving 257 high-Q3 items changes C\\% by $<$0.5\\,pp.\n\n\\paragraph{Model non-independence.}\nGraduated de-duplication (capping models per family at $K=\\{1,2,5,10,20\\}$) shows C\\% $\\leq$ random-subsample C\\% at every cap: $K=20$ yields 29.1\\% vs.\\ random 30.9\\%; $K=5$ yields 11.7\\% vs.\\ random 16.9\\%.\nFamily structure does not inflate C\\%.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT $\\theta$-conditioning.}\nReplacing CTT total-score matching with IRT $\\theta$-conditioning ($r=0.9986$ between $\\theta$ and total score) yields identical results: temporal FPR$=$0.312 vs.\\ 0.314 with CTT.\n\n\\paragraph{Non-uniform DIF.}\nLogistic-regression DIF on 9 subjects (1,787 items) shows 92.1\\% concordance with MH uniform classifications.\nNon-uniform DIF adds 24.8\\% (effect-size controlled), making 31\\% a conservative lower bound.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Logistic regression models predicting DIF-C classification. OR per 0.1-unit change. Simpson's paradox: domain $\\Delta$acc reverses from OR$=$1.55 to OR$=$0.73 when item properties are included.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrl}\n\\toprule\nModel & Predictors & $R^2_{\\text{McF}}$ & Key OR [95\\% CI] \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 0.0007 & 1.55 [1.19, 2.03] \\\\\nM2 & M1 + diff.\\ + disc.\\ & 0.0423 & 0.73 [0.55, 0.97] \\\\\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.0012 & 1.08 \\\\\nM4 & M3 + diff.\\ + disc.\\ & 0.0434 & 0.92 [0.69, 1.22] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:regression} presents the item-level decomposition.\nDomain accuracy improvement shows a marginal association with DIF (M1: OR$=$1.55, $R^2=0.0007$), but this reverses when item difficulty and discrimination are included (M2: OR$=$0.73).\nThis Simpson's paradox confirms that domain-level improvement is not the driver of item-level DIF.\nItem discrimination is the true predictor (OR$=$1.51--1.54), which is psychometrically expected: more discriminating items are more sensitive to group differences.\nEven the full model (M4) explains only 7.4\\% of DIF variance---92.6\\% remains item-specific.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication}\n\\label{sec:crossbench}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks. All show substantial DIF far exceeding random controls ($<$1\\%).}\n\\label{tab:sixbench}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $N_{\\text{items}}$ & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & C\\% & Enrich.\\ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 174$\\times$ \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 & 95$\\times$ \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 & 80$\\times$ \\\\\nARC-Challenge & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 & 45$\\times$ \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 & 63$\\times$ \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 & 64$\\times$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:sixbench} shows temporal DIF across six benchmarks.\nAll exhibit substantial instability (17.9--31.3\\%), with random controls uniformly below 1\\% and enrichment ratios $\\geq$45$\\times$.\nKnowledge-intensive benchmarks (MMLU, TruthfulQA) show higher rates than reasoning benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag), an interpretable pattern consistent with training data vintage effects.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K cross-benchmark analysis. Semi-synthetic ΔTPR confirms framework portability. GSM1K correlation is exploratory ($N=33$).}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llr}\n\\toprule\nAnalysis & Metric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal DIF & C\\% & 18.9 \\\\\nTemporal DIF & Random control C\\% & 0.3 \\\\\nSemi-synthetic & $\\Delta$TPR ($p=1.0$) & 0.818 \\\\\nSemi-synthetic & $\\Delta$FPR & 0.0 \\\\\nGSM1K correlation & Pearson $r$ & $-0.478$ ($p=0.005$) \\\\\nGSM1K correlation & $N$ models & 33 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:gsm8k} details the GSM8K analysis.\nThe semi-synthetic framework achieves $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818 (higher than MMLU's +0.674 due to cleaner single-domain matching).\nGSM1K contamination-gap correlation ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$) is negative---opposite to a simple contamination hypothesis---suggesting DIF captures genuine functioning changes rather than memorization.\nWe note this as exploratory given the small sample ($N=33$, CI: [$-0.71$, $-0.16$]) and leave confirmation to future work.\n\nHalf-year temporal windows (2023-H2 vs.\\ 2024-H1) yield C\\%$=$34.35\\% on MMLU---higher than the full-year rate---indicating DIF saturates within 6 months.\nThis pattern holds across all benchmarks, informing a practical monitoring frequency for audit protocols.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nDirect comparison of DIF-C flags against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items reveals statistical independence: enrichment$=$0.964, Jaccard$=$0.206, $\\phi=0.012$, $\\chi^2=1.28$ ($p=0.258$), OR$=$1.05 [0.96, 1.15].\nDIF flags and web-overlap labels measure fundamentally different constructs: web-overlap captures \\emph{whether} an item is publicly accessible, while DIF captures \\emph{whether} an item functions differently across cohorts.\nEnrichment$\\approx$1.0 is the expected outcome under our meta-critique, not evidence of DIF failure.\n\nThis has field-wide implications: all item-level contamination detectors validated exclusively against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, ConStat, and n-gram methods---have this validation blind spot.\nSemi-synthetic injection confirms DIF \\emph{can} detect differential contamination ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 at $p=0.5$ with cross-subject matching), demonstrating that the method works for its intended purpose even though web-overlap labels cannot verify this.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR and $\\Delta$FPR by matching strategy. Cross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 (Proposition~1).}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$FPR & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.03}$ & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.05}$ & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.10}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nCross-subject & 0.000 & 0.222 & 0.508 & 0.674 \\\\\nWithin-subject & 0.186 & 0.134 & 0.298 & 0.302 \\\\\nCross-benchmark & 0.000 & -- & -- & 0.818 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents the semi-synthetic validation results.\nCross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all 5 tested subjects, empirically confirming Proposition~1.\nWithin-subject matching suffers from substantial FPR inflation (+0.186) due to matching variable contamination, demonstrating that the choice of matching strategy is not merely technical but has fundamental validity implications.\n\nThe dose-response curve is monotonic with inflection at $p \\approx 0.35$ and saturation at $p \\approx 0.7$, providing an operational characterization of DIF sensitivity: moderate contamination ($p=0.5$) is reliably detected, while low-level contamination ($p<0.3$) falls below the detection threshold.\n\nCross-benchmark portability is confirmed by GSM8K replication ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818, $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000), establishing the framework as a reusable validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector on multi-domain benchmarks.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Lens}\n\\label{sec:downstream}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.}\nRemoving all 3,918 DIF-C items yields Spearman $\\rho=0.9965$ with full-benchmark rankings---MMLU has massive measurement redundancy.\nProgressive removal analysis confirms graceful degradation: $\\rho=0.9998$ at 5\\%, 0.9976 at 20\\%, and 0.9891 even at 50\\% removal.\nCronbach's $\\alpha$ drops from 0.994 to 0.990 at 31\\% removal; construct coverage remains at 96\\%.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF items carry cross-cohort signal.}\nDIF removal is more disruptive than random removal across all 21 conditions (7 removal fractions $\\times$ 3 metrics; mean $z=-14.91$).\nHowever, circularity ablation shows DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal is 16$\\times$ more disruptive ($z=-157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance 11$\\times$ ($z=-108$), and high-discrimination 1.7$\\times$ ($z=-16$).\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning isolates genuine differential functioning rather than raw group differences (DIF-discrimination Jaccard$=$0.12).\n\n\\paragraph{Item characterization.}\nDIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d=-0.247$) and higher-discrimination ($d=0.230$) but uniformly distributed across domains ($\\chi^2$ $p=0.32$).\nThe key finding is a difficulty$\\times$direction interaction ($\\rho=-0.597$): 88.7\\% of hardest-decile DIF items favor 2024 models, while 96.2\\% of easiest-decile items favor 2023 models.\nThis reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF-cleaned benchmark demonstration.}\nRemoving 3,918 DIF-C items from MMLU produces systematic cohort asymmetry ($z=8.5$ vs.\\ random baseline $z<1.0$): 2024 models lose an average of 73.6 ranks, while 2023 models gain 22.6.\nIndividual model impacts are substantial (phi-3: $-$258 ranks; mistral derivatives: $+$60).\nThis demonstrates that instability, while not threatening overall ranking validity, introduces measurable cohort-specific bias---precisely the diagnostic signal that benchmark maintainers need.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 5. LIMITATIONS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\nOur analysis has three main limitations.\nFirst, 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (RMSEA$>$0.08).\nOur attempt at bifactor modeling failed due to inadequate fit, motivating future multidimensional IRT work.\nSecond, the semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination processes; DIF detection power for subtle contamination patterns remains uncharacterized.\nThird, reasoning-RL robustness is supported only by directional evidence ($N=13$ RL models added to $N=820$ cohort, $\\Delta$C\\%$=$0.1\\,pp) and theoretical arguments, not by a powered empirical test.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 6. CONCLUSION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks, revealing that 31\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations.\nThis instability is genuine---not an artifact of capability improvement, architecture heterogeneity, model non-independence, or statistical procedure---and replicates across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\nThe field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0) provides the needed validation infrastructure.\nDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: benchmark maintainers can identify which items and capability dimensions are shifting, enabling principled benchmark maintenance without sacrificing measurement validity.\n\n% ============================================================\n% REFERENCES (placeholder)\n% ============================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et~al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021mmlu}\nD.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988alternate}\nP.~W. Holland and D.~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {M}antel--{H}aenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Wainer(1993)]{holland1993differential}\nP.~W. Holland and H.~Wainer.\n\\newblock \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}.\n\\newblock Lawrence Erlbaum, 1993.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nY.~Li, Y.~Dong, R.~Xiao, D.~Yin, and Y.~Zhang.\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nY.~Perlitz, E.~Bandel, and L.~Ein-Dor.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale benchmark of model evaluations.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.10108}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024growing}\nF.~M. Polo, R.~Fonseca, L.~Weber, and others.\n\\newblock Growing pains: On the scalability of adaptive evaluation.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993practical}\nM.~Zieky.\n\\newblock Practical questions in the use of {DIF} statistics in test development.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1993.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n% ============================================================\n% APPENDIX\n% ============================================================\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Experiments}\n\\label{app:supplementary}\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness (\\S\\ref{sec:confounds}).}\nAdding 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) to the focal cohort changes C\\% by only 0.1\\,pp (31.2\\% vs.\\ 31.1\\% baseline; Jaccard$=$0.94).\nTraditional detectors degrade substantially on RL models (Min-K\\% AUROC: $0.72 \\to 0.51$, DeLong $p=0.03$; ConStat: $0.68 \\to 0.43$, $p=0.008$), while DIF remains stable (AUROC: $0.92 \\to 0.91$, $p=0.88$).\nPower analysis shows the partial-exploitation detection boundary at $p \\approx 0.45$.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\nIFT analysis with continuous release-date covariates yields 33.8\\% detection rate (Jaccard$=$0.78, $\\kappa=0.74$ with MH).\nDIF onset concentrates in 2023-Q4 ($\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ increases 0.08/month with inflection at 2023-Q4), suggesting training-data-cutoff-related temporal boundaries.\n\n\\paragraph{Graceful degradation.}\nProgressive item removal shows $\\rho \\geq 0.997$ at 31\\% and $\\geq 0.989$ at 50\\%.\nDIF-targeted removal is slightly more disruptive than random ($z=-9.5$) but well within the theoretical bound of $1-(r/N)^2$.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative Bayesian DIF (simulation).}\nAcross 4 simulated administrations ($N=200$ per cohort per wave), TPR for medium effects ($\\delta=1.0$) grows from 0.40 (wave~1) to 0.78 (wave~4), while FPR remains stable ($\\leq$0.045).\nBayes factors accumulate from median$=$3.2 (wave~1) to 28.5 (wave~3), demonstrating the practical viability of longitudinal DIF monitoring.\n\n\\paragraph{Killed/blocked experiments.}\nBifactor IRT equating (RMSEA$>$0.08, CFI$<$0.90) failed due to MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality.\nItem generation calibration was blocked by bifactor failure.\nEnsemble fusion (DIF$+$ConStat$+$DVD) was dropped with the pivot from detection to characterization.\nThese negative results motivated the diagnostic-lens framing adopted in this paper.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2683,"cache_read_input_tokens":54853,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2683},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a96ac6f-0028-4dc7-a84c-c282fab56a59","uuid":"962d5dc5-a87b-4405-b4d8-e2842a6921c4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1jqmNyAiPTgFE9pBQZMqE","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/best/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a96ac6f-0028-4dc7-a84c-c282fab56a59","uuid":"da54ed20-19d0-4b56-ab22-89ef2bb0244a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:45:12.330Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T141728/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n% ============================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume that item difficulty is stable across model generations---when we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we presume both scores measure the same construct.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that \\textbf{31.3\\%} of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts---a rate 142$\\times$ above the permutation null ($p<0.001$, $z=622$).\nThis instability is robust across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%), not explained by capability improvement ($\\leq$2.4\\,pp of C\\%; $R^2=0.0007$), and not driven by model non-independence or architecture heterogeneity.\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection (enrichment $\\approx$1.0, $\\chi^2$ $p=0.258$), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all tested subjects.\nDespite 31\\% instability, MMLU rankings remain robust ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), but DIF-item removal reveals systematic cohort bias ($z=8.5$)---DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding \\emph{which} capability dimensions shift across generations, not a quality filter.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n% ============================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nLLM evaluation relies heavily on static benchmarks whose items are assumed to function identically regardless of when or which model is evaluated.\nThis assumption---\\emph{temporal measurement invariance}---is rarely tested, yet its violation would undermine cross-generational score comparability.\nPsychometrics has a mature tool for exactly this purpose: \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\citep{holland1993differential}.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to METABENCH \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, a repository of model-level response matrices, analyzing 5,227 models on 12,508 MMLU \\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} items.\nThis is the largest DIF analysis conducted on LLM benchmarks.\nOur key narrative principle: \\textbf{instability $\\neq$ invalidity}.\nDIF flags items whose functioning changed across model generations---this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict.\n\nWe make four contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Temporal instability characterization}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large DIF between 2023 and 2024 cohorts, robust to threshold choice (10--49\\%), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3,918), and systematic confound elimination (architecture, ability gap, local dependence, model non-independence).\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Their enrichment $\\approx$1.0 against DIF flags is expected, not failure. Any item-level detector validated against these labels has this blind spot.\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818).\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z=-9.5$ to $-49\\sigma$) but are the least disruptive non-random removal strategy. Removing DIF items reveals systematic cohort bias ($z=8.5$), demonstrating actionable diagnostics for benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n% ============================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nToken-level methods (Min-K\\%: AUROC$=$0.72 on SFT, degrading to 0.51 on reasoning-RL models) and statistical approaches (ConStat: AUROC$=$0.62; DVD: AUROC$=$0.58--0.71) detect memorization but target different signals than DIF.\nConStat's perturbation-sensitivity approach and DVD's generation-variance method are orthogonal to DIF's cohort-differential signal by construction (Jaccard overlap$=$0.15 between DIF and ConStat flags).\n\n\\paragraph{Web-overlap ground truth.}\n\\citet{li2024contamination} provide web-overlap labels for MMLU items, classifying whether items appear in public training corpora.\nThese labels have become the \\emph{de facto} ground truth for contamination detection validation, but they measure global exposure rather than differential contamination---a distinction we formalize in \\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.}\nMH-DIF \\citep{mantel1959statistical,holland1988alternate} is the standard procedure for detecting items that function differently across demographic groups in high-stakes testing.\nThe ETS classification system (A/B/C) combines statistical significance with effect-size thresholds \\citep{zieky1993practical}.\nWe apply this framework without modification to LLM model cohorts, treating release date as the grouping variable.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark evaluation methodology.}\nGrowing Pains \\citep{polo2024growing} uses anchor-item designs for efficient LLM evaluation.\nOur DIF analysis is complementary: it identifies which anchor items are degrading over time.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{MH-DIF for LLM Benchmarks}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times I}$ (models $\\times$ items), we partition models into reference (2023) and focal (2024-H1) cohorts.\nFor each item $i$, the Mantel--Haenszel statistic tests whether the odds of correct response differ between cohorts after stratifying by total score (ability proxy).\nThe MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ is converted to the $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ effect-size metric on the ETS delta scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified per ETS guidelines: Category~A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|<1.0$ or not significant), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ and significant), C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$ and significant).\nCategory~C items exhibit large DIF warranting review.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\nTo validate DIF detection power under controlled conditions, we inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor a subset of focal-cohort models and target items, we flip incorrect responses to correct with probability $p$ (exploitation rate), simulating contamination at controlled intensity.\nWe measure $\\Delta$TPR $=$ TPR$(p)$ $-$ TPR$(0)$ and $\\Delta$FPR $=$ FPR$(p)$ $-$ FPR$(0)$.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}\n\\label{sec:crosssubject}\n\nStandard MH-DIF uses total score as the ability matching variable.\nWhen contamination affects the same items used for matching, the matching variable itself becomes contaminated, inflating FPR.\nWe propose \\emph{cross-subject matching}: for items in subject $s$, use total score on all subjects \\emph{except} $s$ as the matching variable.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination at the cost of slightly noisier ability estimation.\n\n\\textbf{Proposition 1.} Under cross-subject matching, injecting contamination into subject $s$ items does not alter the matching variable for those items.\nTherefore $\\Delta$FPR $= 0$ for the contaminated subject, regardless of exploitation rate $p$.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal}\n\nWe analyze the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) with temporal cohorts: reference ($N_{\\text{ref}}=1{,}080$, released before 2024) and focal ($N_{\\text{foc}}=807$, released 2024-H1).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF summary on MMLU. C\\% denotes the percentage of items classified as ETS Category~C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). Random C\\% is from within-cohort random splits.}\n\\label{tab:temporal}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nCohort & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & A\\% & B\\% & C\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal 2023 vs.\\ 2024 & 1,080 & 807 & 45.3 & 23.4 & 31.3 \\\\\nAbility Q1 vs.\\ Q5 & 2,608 & 2,608 & 82.1 & 15.0 & 2.9 \\\\\nWithin-family Llama-2 vs.\\ 3 & 161 & 308 & 18.7 & 20.0 & 61.3 \\\\\n\\midrule\nRandom split (sanity) & -- & -- & -- & -- & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal} presents the primary results.\nThe temporal cohort shows 31.3\\% C-rate items (3,918 items; 95\\% bootstrap CI: [29.5, 33.1], $n=1{,}000$).\nThe random-split sanity check yields C\\%$=$0.18\\%, confirming that the MH procedure itself does not inflate false positives.\nThe ability-quintile cohort (Q1 vs.\\ Q5) shows only 2.9\\% C-rate (enrichment vs.\\ local independence$=$1.19), demonstrating that raw ability differences alone produce minimal DIF.\nThe within-family comparison (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3, $d=-0.99$) yields 61.3\\%, providing an informative contrast for the ability-confound analysis in \\S\\ref{sec:confounds}.\n\nAll 57 MMLU subjects exhibit DIF (C\\% range: 20.7--48.4\\%, mean$=$30.6\\%, $\\text{SD}=5.5$; Kruskal--Wallis $p=0.78$), confirming benchmark-wide instability rather than subject-specific artifacts.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Domain-level DIF distribution (top/bottom subjects by C\\%).}\n\\label{tab:domain}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}\n\\toprule\nSubject & $N$ & C\\% & \\%Fav.\\ 2024 & Domain \\\\\n\\midrule\nbusiness\\_ethics & 100 & 48.4 & 38.0 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\nhuman\\_aging & 223 & 41.9 & 34.5 & Other \\\\\nformal\\_logic & 126 & 40.4 & 42.1 & Humanities \\\\\nhigh\\_school\\_stats & 216 & 42.0 & 55.3 & STEM \\\\\n\\midrule\ncomputer\\_security & 100 & 20.7 & 48.1 & STEM \\\\\nmarketing & 234 & 20.9 & 40.2 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\nmanagement & 103 & 21.7 & 43.7 & Social Sci.\\ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:domain} shows that high-DIF and low-DIF subjects span all domains.\nA modest subject-level correlation exists between DIF rate and domain accuracy improvement ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$, $n=57$), but item-level decomposition (\\S\\ref{sec:confounds}) shows this is largely a Simpson's paradox artifact.\n\nSTEM subjects show higher rates of 2024-favoring DIF (55.4\\%) versus Humanities (35.6\\%), revealing domain-specific capability evolution patterns.\n\n\\subsection{Permutation Null and Statistical Robustness}\n\\label{sec:permutation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Permutation null distribution ($N=1{,}000$) vs.\\ observed temporal C\\%.}\n\\label{tab:permutation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nObserved C\\% & 31.3 \\\\\nPermutation null mean & 0.22 \\\\\nPermutation null SD & 0.05 \\\\\nPermutation null max & 0.41 \\\\\n$z$-score & 622.0 \\\\\n$p$-value & $<0.001$ \\\\\nRandom-split control & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe randomly reassign temporal labels 1,000 times (preserving $N_{\\text{ref}}=1{,}080$, $N_{\\text{foc}}=807$) and rerun MH-DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:permutation}).\nThe observed 31.3\\% never appears in any permutation (null max$=$0.41\\%), yielding $z=622$ ($p<0.001$).\nThe null mean (0.22\\%) closely matches the random-split control (0.18\\%), confirming both as valid null estimates.\n\nThreshold sensitivity analysis shows C\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ thresholds \\{1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5\\} $=$ \\{48.9\\%, 31.3\\%, 18.3\\%, 10.2\\%\\}.\nBenjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q=0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C items (31.32\\%$\\to$31.30\\%), confirming that the ETS dual requirement (effect size $+$ significance) provides near-complete implicit multiple-testing protection.\n\n\\subsection{Systematic Confound Elimination}\n\\label{sec:confounds}\n\nWe systematically evaluate six potential confounds.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture heterogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227).\nRestricting to decoder-only models yields identical results (C\\%$=$31.3\\%, Jaccard$=$1.0, 0 items changed).\n\n\\paragraph{Ability confound.}\nThe temporal cohort ability gap is small (Cohen's $d=-0.17$, overlap$=$76.4\\%).\nNearest-neighbor 1:1 matching ($d \\approx -0.01$) reduces C\\% from 31.3\\% to 28.9\\%$\\pm$0.1\\%---ability confound accounts for only 2.4\\,pp (7.7\\%).\nThe within-family Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 contrast ($d=-0.99$) drops from 61.3\\% to 28.3\\% under matching.\nBoth analyses converge to $\\sim$28--29\\% genuine item-level DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Local independence.}\nYen's Q3 statistics show negligible local dependence (mean Q3$=$0.009 within-subject, 2.2\\% of cross-subject pairs above 0.20).\nRemoving 257 high-Q3 items changes C\\% by $<$0.5\\,pp.\n\n\\paragraph{Model non-independence.}\nGraduated de-duplication (capping models per family at $K=\\{1,2,5,10,20\\}$) shows C\\% $\\leq$ random-subsample C\\% at every cap: $K=20$ yields 29.1\\% vs.\\ random 30.9\\%; $K=5$ yields 11.7\\% vs.\\ random 16.9\\%.\nFamily structure does not inflate C\\%.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT $\\theta$-conditioning.}\nReplacing CTT total-score matching with IRT $\\theta$-conditioning ($r=0.9986$ between $\\theta$ and total score) yields identical results: temporal FPR$=$0.312 vs.\\ 0.314 with CTT.\n\n\\paragraph{Non-uniform DIF.}\nLogistic-regression DIF on 9 subjects (1,787 items) shows 92.1\\% concordance with MH uniform classifications.\nNon-uniform DIF adds 24.8\\% (effect-size controlled), making 31\\% a conservative lower bound.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Logistic regression models predicting DIF-C classification. OR per 0.1-unit change. Simpson's paradox: domain $\\Delta$acc reverses from OR$=$1.55 to OR$=$0.73 when item properties are included.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrl}\n\\toprule\nModel & Predictors & $R^2_{\\text{McF}}$ & Key OR [95\\% CI] \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 0.0007 & 1.55 [1.19, 2.03] \\\\\nM2 & M1 + diff.\\ + disc.\\ & 0.0423 & 0.73 [0.55, 0.97] \\\\\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.0012 & 1.08 \\\\\nM4 & M3 + diff.\\ + disc.\\ & 0.0434 & 0.92 [0.69, 1.22] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:regression} presents the item-level decomposition.\nDomain accuracy improvement shows a marginal association with DIF (M1: OR$=$1.55, $R^2=0.0007$), but this reverses when item difficulty and discrimination are included (M2: OR$=$0.73).\nThis Simpson's paradox confirms that domain-level improvement is not the driver of item-level DIF.\nItem discrimination is the true predictor (OR$=$1.51--1.54), which is psychometrically expected: more discriminating items are more sensitive to group differences.\nEven the full model (M4) explains only 7.4\\% of DIF variance---92.6\\% remains item-specific.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication}\n\\label{sec:crossbench}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks. All show substantial DIF far exceeding random controls ($<$1\\%).}\n\\label{tab:sixbench}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $N_{\\text{items}}$ & $N_{\\text{ref}}$ & $N_{\\text{foc}}$ & C\\% & Enrich.\\ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 & 174$\\times$ \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 & 95$\\times$ \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 & 80$\\times$ \\\\\nARC-Challenge & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 & 45$\\times$ \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 & 63$\\times$ \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 & 64$\\times$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:sixbench} shows temporal DIF across six benchmarks.\nAll exhibit substantial instability (17.9--31.3\\%), with random controls uniformly below 1\\% and enrichment ratios $\\geq$45$\\times$.\nKnowledge-intensive benchmarks (MMLU, TruthfulQA) show higher rates than reasoning benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag), an interpretable pattern consistent with training data vintage effects.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K cross-benchmark analysis. Semi-synthetic ΔTPR confirms framework portability. GSM1K correlation is exploratory ($N=33$).}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llr}\n\\toprule\nAnalysis & Metric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal DIF & C\\% & 18.9 \\\\\nTemporal DIF & Random control C\\% & 0.3 \\\\\nSemi-synthetic & $\\Delta$TPR ($p=1.0$) & 0.818 \\\\\nSemi-synthetic & $\\Delta$FPR & 0.0 \\\\\nGSM1K correlation & Pearson $r$ & $-0.478$ ($p=0.005$) \\\\\nGSM1K correlation & $N$ models & 33 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:gsm8k} details the GSM8K analysis.\nThe semi-synthetic framework achieves $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818 (higher than MMLU's +0.674 due to cleaner single-domain matching).\nGSM1K contamination-gap correlation ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$) is negative---opposite to a simple contamination hypothesis---suggesting DIF captures genuine functioning changes rather than memorization.\nWe note this as exploratory given the small sample ($N=33$, CI: [$-0.71$, $-0.16$]) and leave confirmation to future work.\n\nHalf-year temporal windows (2023-H2 vs.\\ 2024-H1) yield C\\%$=$34.35\\% on MMLU---higher than the full-year rate---indicating DIF saturates within 6 months.\nThis pattern holds across all benchmarks, informing a practical monitoring frequency for audit protocols.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nDirect comparison of DIF-C flags against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items reveals statistical independence: enrichment$=$0.964, Jaccard$=$0.206, $\\phi=0.012$, $\\chi^2=1.28$ ($p=0.258$), OR$=$1.05 [0.96, 1.15].\nDIF flags and web-overlap labels measure fundamentally different constructs: web-overlap captures \\emph{whether} an item is publicly accessible, while DIF captures \\emph{whether} an item functions differently across cohorts.\nEnrichment$\\approx$1.0 is the expected outcome under our meta-critique, not evidence of DIF failure.\n\nThis has field-wide implications: all item-level contamination detectors validated exclusively against web-overlap labels---including Min-K\\%, ConStat, and n-gram methods---have this validation blind spot.\nSemi-synthetic injection confirms DIF \\emph{can} detect differential contamination ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 at $p=0.5$ with cross-subject matching), demonstrating that the method works for its intended purpose even though web-overlap labels cannot verify this.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\\label{sec:validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR and $\\Delta$FPR by matching strategy. Cross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 (Proposition~1).}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMatching & $\\Delta$FPR & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.03}$ & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.05}$ & $\\Delta$TPR$_{.10}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nCross-subject & 0.000 & 0.222 & 0.508 & 0.674 \\\\\nWithin-subject & 0.186 & 0.134 & 0.298 & 0.302 \\\\\nCross-benchmark & 0.000 & -- & -- & 0.818 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents the semi-synthetic validation results.\nCross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000 across all 5 tested subjects, empirically confirming Proposition~1.\nWithin-subject matching suffers from substantial FPR inflation (+0.186) due to matching variable contamination, demonstrating that the choice of matching strategy is not merely technical but has fundamental validity implications.\n\nThe dose-response curve is monotonic with inflection at $p \\approx 0.35$ and saturation at $p \\approx 0.7$, providing an operational characterization of DIF sensitivity: moderate contamination ($p=0.5$) is reliably detected, while low-level contamination ($p<0.3$) falls below the detection threshold.\n\nCross-benchmark portability is confirmed by GSM8K replication ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818, $\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000), establishing the framework as a reusable validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector on multi-domain benchmarks.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Lens}\n\\label{sec:downstream}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.}\nRemoving all 3,918 DIF-C items yields Spearman $\\rho=0.9965$ with full-benchmark rankings---MMLU has massive measurement redundancy.\nProgressive removal analysis confirms graceful degradation: $\\rho=0.9998$ at 5\\%, 0.9976 at 20\\%, and 0.9891 even at 50\\% removal.\nCronbach's $\\alpha$ drops from 0.994 to 0.990 at 31\\% removal; construct coverage remains at 96\\%.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF items carry cross-cohort signal.}\nDIF removal is more disruptive than random removal across all 21 conditions (7 removal fractions $\\times$ 3 metrics; mean $z=-14.91$).\nHowever, circularity ablation shows DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal is 16$\\times$ more disruptive ($z=-157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance 11$\\times$ ($z=-108$), and high-discrimination 1.7$\\times$ ($z=-16$).\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning isolates genuine differential functioning rather than raw group differences (DIF-discrimination Jaccard$=$0.12).\n\n\\paragraph{Item characterization.}\nDIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d=-0.247$) and higher-discrimination ($d=0.230$) but uniformly distributed across domains ($\\chi^2$ $p=0.32$).\nThe key finding is a difficulty$\\times$direction interaction ($\\rho=-0.597$): 88.7\\% of hardest-decile DIF items favor 2024 models, while 96.2\\% of easiest-decile items favor 2023 models.\nThis reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF-cleaned benchmark demonstration.}\nRemoving 3,918 DIF-C items from MMLU produces systematic cohort asymmetry ($z=8.5$ vs.\\ random baseline $z<1.0$): 2024 models lose an average of 73.6 ranks, while 2023 models gain 22.6.\nIndividual model impacts are substantial (phi-3: $-$258 ranks; mistral derivatives: $+$60).\nThis demonstrates that instability, while not threatening overall ranking validity, introduces measurable cohort-specific bias---precisely the diagnostic signal that benchmark maintainers need.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 5. LIMITATIONS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\nOur analysis has three main limitations.\nFirst, 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (RMSEA$>$0.08).\nOur attempt at bifactor modeling failed due to inadequate fit, motivating future multidimensional IRT work.\nSecond, the semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination processes; DIF detection power for subtle contamination patterns remains uncharacterized.\nThird, reasoning-RL robustness is supported only by directional evidence ($N=13$ RL models added to $N=820$ cohort, $\\Delta$C\\%$=$0.1\\,pp) and theoretical arguments, not by a powered empirical test.\n\n% ============================================================\n% 6. CONCLUSION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks, revealing that 31\\% of MMLU items are temporally unstable across model generations.\nThis instability is genuine---not an artifact of capability improvement, architecture heterogeneity, model non-independence, or statistical procedure---and replicates across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\nThe field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0) provides the needed validation infrastructure.\nDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens: benchmark maintainers can identify which items and capability dimensions are shifting, enabling principled benchmark maintenance without sacrificing measurement validity.\n\n% ============================================================\n% REFERENCES (placeholder)\n% ============================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et~al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021mmlu}\nD.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988alternate}\nP.~W. Holland and D.~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {M}antel--{H}aenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Wainer(1993)]{holland1993differential}\nP.~W. Holland and H.~Wainer.\n\\newblock \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}.\n\\newblock Lawrence Erlbaum, 1993.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nY.~Li, Y.~Dong, R.~Xiao, D.~Yin, and Y.~Zhang.\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel and Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nY.~Perlitz, E.~Bandel, and L.~Ein-Dor.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale benchmark of model evaluations.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.10108}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024growing}\nF.~M. Polo, R.~Fonseca, L.~Weber, and others.\n\\newblock Growing pains: On the scalability of adaptive evaluation.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993practical}\nM.~Zieky.\n\\newblock Practical questions in the use of {DIF} statistics in test development.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}, pages 337--347. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1993.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n% ============================================================\n% APPENDIX\n% ============================================================\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Experiments}\n\\label{app:supplementary}\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness (\\S\\ref{sec:confounds}).}\nAdding 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) to the focal cohort changes C\\% by only 0.1\\,pp (31.2\\% vs.\\ 31.1\\% baseline; Jaccard$=$0.94).\nTraditional detectors degrade substantially on RL models (Min-K\\% AUROC: $0.72 \\to 0.51$, DeLong $p=0.03$; ConStat: $0.68 \\to 0.43$, $p=0.008$), while DIF remains stable (AUROC: $0.92 \\to 0.91$, $p=0.88$).\nPower analysis shows the partial-exploitation detection boundary at $p \\approx 0.45$.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\nIFT analysis with continuous release-date covariates yields 33.8\\% detection rate (Jaccard$=$0.78, $\\kappa=0.74$ with MH).\nDIF onset concentrates in 2023-Q4 ($\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ increases 0.08/month with inflection at 2023-Q4), suggesting training-data-cutoff-related temporal boundaries.\n\n\\paragraph{Graceful degradation.}\nProgressive item removal shows $\\rho \\geq 0.997$ at 31\\% and $\\geq 0.989$ at 50\\%.\nDIF-targeted removal is slightly more disruptive than random ($z=-9.5$) but well within the theoretical bound of $1-(r/N)^2$.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative Bayesian DIF (simulation).}\nAcross 4 simulated administrations ($N=200$ per cohort per wave), TPR for medium effects ($\\delta=1.0$) grows from 0.40 (wave~1) to 0.78 (wave~4), while FPR remains stable ($\\leq$0.045).\nBayes factors accumulate from median$=$3.2 (wave~1) to 28.5 (wave~3), demonstrating the practical viability of longitudinal DIF monitoring.\n\n\\paragraph{Killed/blocked experiments.}\nBifactor IRT equating (RMSEA$>$0.08, CFI$<$0.90) failed due to MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality.\nItem generation calibration was blocked by bifactor failure.\nEnsemble fusion (DIF$+$ConStat$+$DVD) was dropped with the pivot from detection to characterization.\nThese negative results motivated the diagnostic-lens framing adopted in this paper.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
